{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision on 4dccae54: the capacity runtime choices for 113a54bd. These settle the four open choices for slices 1 to 3 of the capacity preparation note (notes/implementation/2026-10-17-capacity-guarantees-preparation.md). They add no reason code to act refusals, and they change no existing scope and no existing replay.\n\n1. Opt-in and version. A definition opts in with an optional top-level field `capacity: {name: \"whole-scope\", version: 1}`. Validate it the way `level` is validated (packages/derive/src/validate/index.ts:157-170, the A0a precedent). If the field is omitted, today's entries-only admission and replay apply and the definition's bytes are unchanged. Any other name or version is refused `unsupported-definition`. Admission and replay read the pinned definition, so each scope keeps one rule for its whole life. Nothing changes admission or replay globally.\n\n2. The four ceilings. Add four deployment Bounds beside scopeEntries, with the same status as scopeEntries (contract section 9.2: temporary values, owed to R4 measurement, and no promise about physical storage). They are scopeBytes 1,073,741,824 (1 GiB), scopeItems 100,000, scopeRecords 200,000 and scopeRequests 10,000 (pending requests). They apply only to opted-in scopes. Their comments say they are temporary. Witnesses inject small values. If the name scopeBytes is confused with the SQLite metadata reader scopeBytes(), rename the reader.\n\n3. Units at write points. Use the units already adopted (78b6d129; contract r23 section 17.1):\n- A first key counts one. Replacing an existing key counts none.\n- Bytes count once per byte domain and digest.\n- A redaction removes bytes from the redaction entry onward.\nUsed must be derivable from stored state. A counter persisted in the same transaction is a cache; restart and replay check it against the derivation.\n\nProducer maxima: a reservation uses the existing bound that already limits that form. For example, entryBytes limits a retained foreign entry, definitionBytes a retained definition, and the declared value bounds a value. Where no existing bound limits a producer, validation of an opted-in definition refuses the definition with a problem that names the producer (code `reserve-unbounded`). Such a producer is never counted as zero. Its owner declares a finite maximum as separate work. In the slice note, list the producers that the journey's rooms reach.\n\nHistorical bytes: replay of an opted-in scope checks bytes at every position where the counted bytes are available. A later redaction may have removed a text whose size was not kept. For that span, replay reports the byte check as `incomplete`, with the span and the reason \"redacted size not retained\". This is neither a pass nor a failure. The other three dimensions are still checked there. Runtime admission never needs historical sizes, because it reads the current Used.\n\n4. Refusal report. Follow the existing entry-too-large convention (packages/contract/src/result.ts:31, \"The name is the bound's\"). A capacity refusal is `{reason: \"scope-full\", name: \u003cbound name\u003e}`. The name is one of scopeEntries, scopeBytes, scopeItems, scopeRecords or scopeRequests. Pass the bound through the callback as `full(head, bound)`. Update the comment on `name` (result.ts:69) to say that it carries either a guard's declared reason or a bound's name. No form recorded in an entry changes: a diagnosis that is written keeps its current bytes. The dimension appears in the answer, the metrics and observability. Refusals write nothing, so scope-full answers for older scopes may also carry the name scopeEntries.\n\n5. Release (coordination f45f1111, item 4). Only the entry that settles a duty releases that duty's reservation. A duty with no reachable final branch keeps its reservation, so the scope fills but never overflows. Never release a reservation because an item looks final.\n\nOrder: send the views design head to review. Then build capacity slices 1 to 3 against these decisions, one dimension at a time. Next come archive A2 and A3 on fixtures, then J3 with the evidence-rules slice. Plan 032 stops capacity when all four dimensions are judged for opted-in scopes, with the witnesses the preparation note lists. Physical storage measurement, measured numbers and producer maxima beyond the existing bounds are outside that stop point.","body":{"decision":"capacity opt-in field version 1; four temporary deployment ceilings; adopted units with incomplete historical byte report; scope-full with bound name; release only by settling entry","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4dccae54d2ae0f5f0fb0433ed7bf90c4a44e5641","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}